== Does the Inertia of a Body Depend upon its Energy-Content?

#text(size: 8.5pt, fill: rgb("#6a6155"), tracking: 0.12em)[
  ADDENDUM · RECEIVED 27 SEPTEMBER 1905
]
#v(0.4em)

The further communication promised above can now be given. Let a body at rest
give off the energy $L$ in the form of radiation, half to each of two opposite
directions. Reckoning the same process from a frame in uniform motion, the
postulates of the June paper balance the books only if the body's mass has
diminished by

$ m = L / V^2 $

where $V$ denotes the velocity of light. The mass of a body is a measure of
its energy-content. In the symbols now usual --- $E$ for the energy and $c$
for $V$ --- the same statement reads

$ E = m c^2 $

It is not excluded that, with bodies whose energy-content is variable to a
high degree --- the salts of radium, say --- the theory may be put to the
test.
